package Vixen.Shaders.PostFx

import Vixen.Shaders.Core
import Vixen.Shaders.Geometry

// Ground-truth ambient occlusion from depth: the horizon-slice integral.
//
// GTAO rather than the classic hemisphere-sampling SSAO, and the difference is not just quality. Classic
// SSAO samples points in a hemisphere and counts how many are occluded, which converges slowly. This
// finds the *horizon angle* on each side of a few screen-space slices and integrates the visible arc
// analytically — so a handful of directions gives a smooth result.
//
// ⚠ **This file used to say all of the above and do something else.** What it ran was the horizon-*based*
// estimator: the largest cosine of a sample's elevation above the tangent plane, averaged over the
// directions and subtracted from one. That is HBAO, and it is not what the comment claimed, not what
// `docs/plan/06`'s table meant by "the full GTAO horizon integral is still to come", and not within a
// constant of the right answer — for a single occluder at elevation `e` it gives `1 - sin(e)/2` where the
// cosine-weighted visibility is `1 - sin²(e)/2`. The two agree at `e = 0` and at `e = 90°` and nowhere
// between: at 45° they are 0.65 and 0.75.
//
// The integral below is the one from Jimenez et al. 2016 § 3.2, and what makes it worth the arithmetic is
// not the accuracy at a horizon but the *normalisation*. Each slice is weighted by how much of the
// surface normal lies in it, and the weights sum so that an unoccluded surface reads exactly one
// whatever direction it faces — at eight directions, to four decimal places, at every tilt. The old
// estimator got that for free by measuring elevation from the tangent plane, and paid for it with a
// horizon term that was wrong everywhere else.
//
// Three things are measured per slice, all of them from the *view vector* rather than from the normal:
//
//   * `h1` and `h2`, the horizon angles either side, as signed angles from the direction of the camera;
//   * `gamma`, the signed angle of the normal projected into the slice plane, from the same direction;
//   * the length of that projection, which is the slice's weight.
//
// The horizons are then clamped into the hemisphere around the projected normal — a surface cannot see
// behind itself, and a horizon found there is a depth sample in front of a *different* surface — and the
// arc between them is integrated in closed form against the cosine lobe.
//
// The bent normal falls out of the same search for free, and is what makes indirect specular respond to
// occlusion rather than only indirect diffuse. ⚠ It is a **view-space** direction, which is the space
// the horizons are found in; the old spelling accumulated screen-space xy against a constant z and was
// not a direction in any space at all.

shader Ssao {
    /// Directions to search. 4 is the mobile setting, 8 the default.
    [Permutation] val Directions: int = 8

    /// Steps along each direction.
    [Permutation] val Steps: int = 6

    /// Whether to output a bent normal alongside the occlusion.
    [Permutation] val BentNormal: bool = false

    var depthBuffer: Texture2D
    var normalBuffer: Texture2D
    var pointSampler: Sampler

    var inverseProjection: mat4

    /// World into view, for the normal. Positions are reconstructed in view space from depth, and
    /// the G-buffer's normal is world-space — dotting the two without this rotation makes the
    /// horizon estimate a function of which way the camera faces.
    var view: mat4

    /// The projection's `[0][0]` and `[1][1]`, which turn a view-space metre into a screen fraction.
    var projectionScale: float2 = float2(1f, 1f)

    var texelSize: float2 = float2(0.001f, 0.001f)

    /// World-space radius the search covers.
    var radius: float = 0.5f

    /// Raised to this power, which is how artists dial occlusion strength.
    var intensity: float = 1f

    /// Distance over which occlusion fades out, so distant geometry does not occlude.
    var falloff: float = 1f

    /// The elevation below which a horizon does not count, as a sine — the self-occlusion guard.
    ///
    /// The march's first sample stands one depth texel out (see `SearchHorizon`), and at one texel
    /// the reconstructed neighbour and the G-buffer's normal disagree by quantisation alone: on a
    /// surface seen at a slant, half a texel of depth rounding reads as a horizon that is not there.
    /// Flooring the elevation is what lets the march sample the near field at all rather than skip
    /// it — a sample that does not clear it is not a horizon and the search moves on.
    ///
    /// ⚠ It floors the *sample*, not the estimate. Under the old estimator this number was
    /// subtracted from the horizon term and the result renormalised, which is why it read as a
    /// strength knob as much as a guard. The integral below has a normalisation of its own that a
    /// second one would fight, so the guard is a rejection and nothing else: raising it lightens
    /// only the contacts, where it used to lighten everything.
    var bias: float = 0.1f

    stream var uv: float2

    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex([Semantic("SV_VertexID")] vertexIndex: int): float4 {
        uv = Fullscreen.Uv(vertexIndex)
        return Fullscreen.Position(vertexIndex)
    }

    [FragmentShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Target")]
    func Fragment(): float4 {
        val centreDepth = depthBuffer.Sample(pointSampler, uv).r
        val centre = Transform.UvDepthToView(inverseProjection, uv, centreDepth)

        // Raw, not unorm: the G-buffer stores the world normal signed in Rgba16Float — see
        // ForwardPlus.targets — and remapping it bends a floor's up into a fixed world diagonal.
        // Rotated into view space, where `centre` and every marched sample live.
        val world = Math.SafeNormalize(normalBuffer.Sample(pointSampler, uv).xyz)
        val n = Math.SafeNormalize((view * float4(world, 0f)).xyz)

        // The radius in UV units shrinks with distance, so the world-space radius is constant — which
        // is what stops AO growing as the camera pulls back. Per axis, through the projection's own
        // scales: a view-space metre is `P00 / (2|z|)` of the screen horizontally and `P11 / (2|z|)`
        // vertically, and without them the kernel is a UV circle — an ellipse in view space that
        // widens with the aspect ratio and never actually matches `radius`.
        val screenRadius = projectionScale * (radius * 0.5f / max(abs(centre.z), Const.Epsilon))

        // Per-pixel rotation, so the few directions do not band. Hashed from the pixel rather than
        // sampled from a noise texture: no binding, and stable across frames.
        val jitter = Random.Float01(Random.Hash2(uint(int(uv.x / texelSize.x)), uint(int(uv.y / texelSize.y))))

        // Toward the camera. Every angle below is measured from this rather than from `n`, which is
        // the whole reason the integral can be written down: a slice's arc is bounded by what the
        // *camera* can see past, and the surface's own orientation enters as `gamma` instead.
        val toCamera = Math.SafeNormalize(-centre)

        var visibility = 0f
        var bent = float3(0f)
        val directionCount = max(Directions, 1)

        for (d in 0 .. directionCount - 1) {
            val angle = (float(d) + jitter) * Const.Pi / float(directionCount)
            val direction = float2(cos(angle), sin(angle))

            // The slice plane, as two view-space vectors.
            //
            // ⚠ The lift negates y. A UV direction of +y walks *down* the screen and view-space +y
            // is up — `Transform.UvToNdc` is where that lives — so a lift that copied the sign
            // mirrors every slice about the horizontal, which flips `gamma` and leaves an image that
            // is smooth, plausible and lit from the wrong side of every crease.
            //
            // Orthogonalised against the view vector rather than assumed perpendicular to it: the
            // two are only perpendicular at the centre of the frame, and at the edges of a wide
            // field of view the error is the difference between a slice plane that contains the
            // camera and one that does not.
            val lifted = float3(direction.x, -direction.y, 0f)
            val slice = Math.SafeNormalize(lifted - toCamera * dot(lifted, toCamera))
            val axis = Math.SafeNormalize(cross(slice, toCamera))

            // The normal projected into the slice, and how much of it landed there. That length is
            // the slice's weight, and it is what makes the whole estimator normalise: a slice the
            // normal barely leans into contributes barely anything, and the weights across the
            // directions sum so that an unoccluded surface reads exactly one at any tilt.
            val projected = n - axis * dot(n, axis)
            val projectedLength = length(projected)

            if (projectedLength > Const.Epsilon) {
                // `gamma`, signed toward `slice`: which way the surface leans within this plane.
                val cosGamma = clamp(dot(projected, toCamera) / projectedLength, -1f, 1f)
                val gamma = Math.SignedOne(dot(projected, slice)) * acos(cosGamma)

                // The horizons, as cosines from the view vector. -1 is "nothing stopped the search",
                // which is the angle straight back past the camera and therefore no occlusion at all.
                val forward = SearchHorizon(centre, n, direction, screenRadius)
                val backward = SearchHorizon(centre, n, -direction, screenRadius)

                // Into signed angles: forward is the `+slice` side by construction.
                var h2 = acos(forward)
                var h1 = -acos(backward)

                // Clamped into the hemisphere around the projected normal. A surface cannot see
                // behind itself, so a horizon found there is a depth sample in front of a *different*
                // surface — the classic halo around a foreground object — and taking it at face value
                // would let one occluder darken past what a full hemisphere of them could.
                h1 = gamma + max(h1 - gamma, -Const.Pi * 0.5f)
                h2 = gamma + min(h2 - gamma, Const.Pi * 0.5f)

                visibility += projectedLength * (Arc(h1, gamma) + Arc(h2, gamma))

                if (BentNormal) {
                    // The middle of what the slice can see, in the slice's own plane. View space,
                    // and weighted the same way the visibility is so the two agree about which
                    // slices mattered.
                    val middle = (h1 + h2) * 0.5f
                    bent += projectedLength * (toCamera * cos(middle) + slice * sin(middle))
                }
            }
        }

        // The directions are slices of one hemisphere and each contributes both of its sides, so the
        // divisor is the direction count and not twice it — `Arc` carries the halving.
        var occlusion = saturate(visibility / float(directionCount))
        occlusion = pow(occlusion, max(intensity, Const.Epsilon))

        if (BentNormal) {
            return float4(Math.ToUnorm(Math.SafeNormalize(bent)), occlusion)
        }

        return float4(occlusion, occlusion, occlusion, 1f)
    }

    /// One side of a slice's visible arc, integrated against the cosine lobe.
    ///
    /// Jimenez et al. 2016 § 3.2. The closed form of `∫ cos(θ - γ)·|sin θ| dθ` from the view vector out
    /// to the horizon at `h`, which is what makes a handful of directions enough: the arc is *solved*
    /// rather than sampled, so the only thing the march has to find is where it ends.
    ///
    /// ⚠ The two sides are separate calls because the `|sin θ|` is what splits them. The absolute value
    /// has a corner at the view vector, so one antiderivative cannot span an arc that crosses it — which
    /// it always does, `h1` being negative and `h2` positive by construction.
    func Arc(h: float, gamma: float): float {
        return 0.25f * (-cos(2f * h - gamma) + cos(gamma) + 2f * h * sin(gamma))
    }

    /// How far the horizon is on one side of a slice, as a cosine from the view vector.
    ///
    /// Marches out along the screen direction and keeps the *largest* cosine seen, which is the sample
    /// that stands highest against the camera and therefore hides everything behind it. `-1` is the
    /// answer when nothing stopped the search: the angle straight back past the camera, which no
    /// hemisphere reaches and which the integral reads as no occlusion at all.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Returns a cosine from the *view vector*, not from the tangent plane. `bias` and the falloff
    /// still speak in elevation above the surface, because that is the frame in which "this sample is
    /// not really an occluder" is a sentence — but what the integral is handed is the camera's angle.
    func SearchHorizon(centre: float3, n: float3, direction: float2, screenRadius: float2): float {
        val toCamera = Math.SafeNormalize(-centre)
        var maxCosine = -1f
        val stepCount = max(Steps, 1)

        // Where the first sample stands. `radius / Steps` out — the even spacing — has a hole in
        // it: anything nearer than the first shell is never sampled, and the nearest occluder a
        // pixel has is the wall it touches. Up close the shell is dozens of texels wide, and the
        // hole reads as an unoccluded strip hugging every corner, exactly one shell thick. So the
        // first sample stands at one *depth texel* instead — the closest thing the buffer resolves
        // — whenever the shell would be farther than that, and the rest spread evenly out to the
        // radius. What the near sample costs is precision noise, which is `bias`'s job above.
        val stride = direction * screenRadius / texelSize
        val first = min(1f / float(stepCount), 1f / max(length(stride), 1f))

        for (s in 1 .. stepCount) {
            val t = first + (1f - first) * float(s - 1) / float(max(stepCount - 1, 1))
            val sampleUv = uv + direction * screenRadius * t

            if (!Fullscreen.InBounds(sampleUv)) {
                break
            }

            val sampleDepth = depthBuffer.Sample(pointSampler, sampleUv).r
            val samplePosition = Transform.UvDepthToView(inverseProjection, sampleUv, sampleDepth)
            val toSample = samplePosition - centre
            val distance = length(toSample)

            val towards = Math.SafeNormalize(toSample)

            // Fades a sample out with distance, so a wall behind the pixel does not occlude it. The
            // fade is toward -1 — no horizon — rather than toward zero, because zero is a real angle
            // here: a cosine of nought from the view vector is an occluder at ninety degrees, which
            // is most of a hemisphere. Fading toward it would make every distant surface a wall.
            val attenuation = saturate(1f - distance / max(radius * falloff, Const.Epsilon))

            // The guard, in the frame it makes sense in: a sample lying almost in the tangent plane
            // is the surface itself under a texel of depth rounding, not something standing on it.
            // The march's first sample is one depth texel out (see above) and at one texel the
            // reconstructed neighbour and the G-buffer's normal disagree by quantisation alone.
            if (dot(towards, n) > bias) {
                maxCosine = max(maxCosine, lerp(-1f, dot(towards, toCamera), attenuation))
            }
        }

        return clamp(maxCosine, -1f, 1f)
    }
}
